+ * C000002D is STATUS_NOT_COMMITTED (include/ntstatus.h:262), *not*
+ * STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES, which is C0000018 (:241). That distinction is
+ * the whole diagnosis. Wine's NtProtectVirtualMemory has exactly three
+ * outcomes (dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c): no view -> STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER,
+ * view found but get_committed_size() short -> STATUS_NOT_COMMITTED, otherwise
+ * success. So the view exists and the page is simply **reserved and not
+ * committed**. Nothing is crossing a boundary and no range is miscomputed --
+ * the reprotect is one page, TmpSize = 1 rounded up, inside a view that exists.
+ *
+ * How a reserved page got into RWXIntervals: this function classifies on Prot
+ * alone, and NotifyMemoryAlloc passed it the Prot of a MEM_RESERVE allocation.
+ * With DisableDEP on (patches/fex/0002, default true) every readable range is
+ * treated as executable and every writable one as SMC-capable, so a 272 MB
+ * MEM_RESERVE of PAGE_READWRITE was recorded as guest RWX code. It is the
+ * FEXOfflineCompiler's own LookupCache: LookupCache.cpp:41 reserves it with
+ * Commit = false and LookupCache.cpp:49 hands it to MarkOvercommitRange, which
+ * is the mechanism that is *supposed* to commit it lazily on first touch.
+ * pc 140088F10 is inside the compiler's own image, which loads at 140000000.
+ *
+ * So the fault was the overcommit design working exactly as intended, and DEP
+ * promotion was stealing it: HandleRWXAccessViolation claimed the address
+ * first, could not reprotect an uncommitted page, reported "handled" anyway,
+ * and resumed -- forever. Zero `Overcommit:` lines in that session is the
+ * corroboration, because the emulation module's OvercommitTracker is empty in
+ * a process with no guest thread; the tracker that owns this range is the
+ * compiler's own, and its handler is downstream of this one.
+ *
+ * **The two sweeps in this file already get this right** -- the constructor at
+ * the top and HandleProcessExecuteFlagsChange both test
+ * `Info.State == MEM_COMMIT` before promoting anything. Only the notification
+ * path did not, because Wine hands the allocation Type to NotifyMemoryAlloc and
+ * NotifyMemoryAlloc dropped it. Passing it through makes the three paths agree.
+ *
+ * This is a classification fix, not a decline: with the reservation kept out of
+ * the interval map the fault is never claimed here at all, and falls through to
+ * the handler that owns the memory and commits it. */
+void InvalidationTracker::HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(uint64_t Address, uint64_t Size, ULONG Prot, bool Committed) {
+  if (!Committed) {
+    // Reserved address space only. Prot describes pages that do not exist yet,
+    // and no code can live in them until something commits them -- at which
+    // point NtAllocateVirtualMemory notifies again with MEM_COMMIT and the
+    // range is classified then, against protections that are real.
+    return;
+  }
+
   const auto AlignedBase = Address & FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_MASK;
   const auto AlignedSize = (Address - AlignedBase + Size + FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE - 1) & FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_MASK;
 
@@ -280,34 +334,53 @@
       SIZE_T TmpSize = 1;
       const NTSTATUS ProtStatus = NtProtectVirtualMemory(NtCurrentProcess(), &TmpAddress, &TmpSize, UntrapProt, &TmpProt);
 
-      /* Vessel: diagnostic only -- this deliberately does NOT change what the
-       * function returns.
+      /* Vessel: 0008 left this as a log line because the branch had not been
+       * shown to loop. It has now -- 13,864,960 consecutive resumes of one page
+       * -- so it becomes the decline that 0008 applied to the overcommit path,
+       * under the same rule: a fault that was not repaired must not be reported
+       * as repaired.
+       *
+       * **Unconditional on failure, not status-specific and not bounded**, and
+       * the reason is that the choice is not really between three policies. The
+       * only thing this function does to make the faulting instruction able to
+       * make progress is the reprotect immediately above. If it failed, the
+       * page is still not writable, so the very same store will fault again the
+       * instant it is resumed -- by construction, for any failure status,
+       * however many times it is retried. Declining after N attempts would
+       * merely make the loop finite; declining on one status would leave the
+       * next status looping. There is no failure mode in which resuming is the
+       * right answer, so there is nothing to condition on.
+       *
+       * **What this cannot regress, and it is worth being precise because the
+       * risk was real enough to defer the change once.** SMC faults on one page
+       * repeating is normal and stays normal: this only fires when the reprotect
+       * *failed*, and on the working path it succeeds. A guest that currently
+       * works cannot be depending on the old behaviour, because the old
+       * behaviour on this branch is an infinite loop rather than a slow path --
+       * "working" and "reached this line" are mutually exclusive today.
        *
-       * This is the other place in the ARM64EC fault path that can answer
-       * "handled" without having changed anything: the reprotect result was
-       * discarded, and on a page that is reserved rather than committed it
